1
00:00:00,667 --> 00:00:04,633
ArkSpec is an operational command
layer for site-based service teams.

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:09,457
The idea is simple: connect the full
operational lifecycle — from

3
00:00:09,457 --> 00:00:12,943
planning and field execution to
evidence, monitoring and

4
00:00:12,943 --> 00:00:15,993
controlled client visibility — without
breaking the context

5
00:00:15,993 --> 00:00:17,300
between those stages.

6
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:22,613
So instead of another isolated
checklist or reporting tool,

7
00:00:22,613 --> 00:00:27,628
ArkSpec keeps the operation itself as
the common thread: Plan.

8
00:00:27,628 --> 00:00:29,633
Execute. Prove. Monitor. Share.

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,262
Most operational teams already have
tools. The problem is that the

10
00:00:36,262 --> 00:00:38,200
operating picture is still fragmented.

11
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:43,143
Instructions and checklists live in
one place, photos somewhere

12
00:00:43,143 --> 00:00:46,986
else, incidents in messages, and
client reporting is often

13
00:00:46,986 --> 00:00:48,267
assembled manually afterwards.

14
00:00:49,367 --> 00:00:53,454
That makes proof difficult to
reconstruct and creates a choice

15
00:00:53,454 --> 00:00:57,541
between sharing too much internal
detail or spending time creating

16
00:00:57,541 --> 00:00:58,767
safe customer updates.

17
00:00:59,267 --> 00:01:02,867
ArkSpec makes the workflow itself the
source of truth.

18
00:01:05,167 --> 00:01:07,400
This is the core product model.

19
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:12,906
We first define sites, zones, teams
and reusable processes. Work

20
00:01:12,906 --> 00:01:16,962
is then executed as assigned or
team-scoped runs. During

21
00:01:16,962 --> 00:01:20,567
execution, the team captures checklist
values and evidence.

22
00:01:21,567 --> 00:01:25,076
Supervisors monitor runs, incidents
and reporting. And when

23
00:01:25,076 --> 00:01:29,024
something needs to be shared
externally, client visibility is

24
00:01:29,024 --> 00:01:32,533
granted deliberately and only selected
information is published.

25
00:01:33,533 --> 00:01:37,219
The important point is that these are
not disconnected modules.

26
00:01:37,219 --> 00:01:40,167
They remain part of the same
operational chain.

27
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:45,833
Here is what that looks like before
work starts.

28
00:01:46,833 --> 00:01:51,284
An organization can model its real
operating structure as sites

29
00:01:51,284 --> 00:01:54,400
and zones, then build reusable process
templates.

30
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:59,189
A stable process version can be
published for live execution while

31
00:01:59,189 --> 00:02:01,600
the next revision remains safely in
draft.

32
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,070
So teams can improve their operating
model without changing the

33
00:02:05,070 --> 00:02:07,833
workflow that is already being used in
the field.

34
00:02:10,100 --> 00:02:13,333
For the worker, the experience becomes
much simpler.

35
00:02:14,333 --> 00:02:18,967
They see the work they can actually
act on, rather than the entire organization.

36
00:02:19,967 --> 00:02:24,647
In this demo, the Worker sees three
scoped runs and five relevant

37
00:02:24,647 --> 00:02:27,767
tasks, while supervisor-only work
stays outside their view.

38
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,841
The interface is responsive for
desktop and mobile. And for

39
00:02:31,841 --> 00:02:36,065
previously loaded tasks, field work
can continue in a limited way

40
00:02:36,065 --> 00:02:39,522
if connectivity drops: checklist
changes and completion can be

41
00:02:39,522 --> 00:02:42,978
queued locally and replayed when
connectivity returns, with normal

42
00:02:42,978 --> 00:02:45,667
server authorization and conflict
checks still applied.

43
00:02:46,667 --> 00:02:51,467
So offline continuity does not mean
bypassing operational controls.

44
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:57,633
The supervisor gets a different
projection of the same operation.

45
00:02:58,633 --> 00:03:02,843
They can review live, completed and
cancelled runs across sites,

46
00:03:02,843 --> 00:03:07,053
see ownership and task state, identify
at-risk or overdue work,

47
00:03:07,053 --> 00:03:10,000
and drill into task-level execution
and evidence.

48
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,825
That means the supervisor does not
have to ask the field team for

49
00:03:14,825 --> 00:03:17,767
a separate status report simply to
understand what is happening.

50
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:23,533
Execution is only one part of the
operating picture.

51
00:03:24,533 --> 00:03:28,156
ArkSpec also keeps incidents, basic
reporting and traceability

52
00:03:28,156 --> 00:03:29,967
connected to the work.

53
00:03:30,967 --> 00:03:35,403
We can surface overdue tasks and
at-risk runs, follow operational

54
00:03:35,403 --> 00:03:38,951
exceptions from investigation to
resolution, and maintain an

55
00:03:38,951 --> 00:03:42,500
organization timeline showing who did
what and when.

56
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:49,333
So the chain remains connected: run,
task, incident, report, audit trail.

57
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:55,233
This is one of the most important
parts of the product.

58
00:03:56,233 --> 00:03:59,967
Internal operational visibility and
client visibility are

59
00:03:59,967 --> 00:04:01,033
deliberately different.

60
00:04:02,033 --> 00:04:06,869
A client is granted access only to
approved locations, and only

61
00:04:06,869 --> 00:04:09,067
specifically published records are
exposed.

62
00:04:09,967 --> 00:04:14,493
In this example, the client sees: one
site, one completed run, two

63
00:04:14,493 --> 00:04:17,133
published evidence items, and one
resolved incident —

64
00:04:18,133 --> 00:04:21,333
without receiving the internal
operational record.

65
00:04:22,333 --> 00:04:25,807
That gives the service provider a way
to create transparency

66
00:04:25,807 --> 00:04:28,933
without simply opening its internal
system to the customer.

67
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:33,707
One note on this demo: the data and
evidence shown here are

68
00:04:33,707 --> 00:04:37,237
illustrative and synthetic, and
selected assets were produced with

69
00:04:37,237 --> 00:04:40,767
AI assistance using ChatGPT. No
customer data is shown.

70
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,833
The same principle applies throughout
ArkSpec.

71
00:04:46,833 --> 00:04:50,376
An administrator, supervisor, worker
and client viewer are

72
00:04:50,376 --> 00:04:53,033
intentionally not shown the same
information.

73
00:04:54,033 --> 00:04:58,278
Workers see assigned or team-scoped
work. Supervisors see the

74
00:04:58,278 --> 00:05:01,107
broader operational picture.
Administrators manage the

75
00:05:01,107 --> 00:05:04,880
organizational model and access.
Clients receive only granted

76
00:05:04,880 --> 00:05:06,767
locations and published content.

77
00:05:07,767 --> 00:05:11,414
Least privilege is therefore part of
the product model, not

78
00:05:11,414 --> 00:05:13,967
something added later as a technical
control.

79
00:05:16,100 --> 00:05:19,933
For a pilot, I would not suggest
trying to digitize everything.

80
00:05:20,933 --> 00:05:24,822
We select one recurring operational
workflow with a clear owner

81
00:05:24,822 --> 00:05:26,767
and a meaningful proof requirement.

82
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:31,131
We model the sites and process, run it
with a small real team,

83
00:05:31,131 --> 00:05:34,627
capture the work and evidence, and
then review the result from

84
00:05:34,627 --> 00:05:36,533
both the supervisor and client
perspectives.

85
00:05:37,533 --> 00:05:41,456
The questions are practical: do
supervisors see problems earlier?

86
00:05:41,456 --> 00:05:45,814
Does evidence reduce follow-up? Does
the worker experience fit the

87
00:05:45,814 --> 00:05:49,300
real workflow? And is client
visibility genuinely useful?

88
00:05:52,067 --> 00:05:53,867
So the next step is deliberately
small.

89
00:05:54,867 --> 00:05:59,188
Choose one real workflow, configure
the pilot operation, run it in

90
00:05:59,188 --> 00:06:03,510
the field and capture the proof, then
review the results together

91
00:06:03,510 --> 00:06:05,867
before making any broader rollout
decision.

92
00:06:06,867 --> 00:06:10,867
The goal is not to prove that ArkSpec
can contain every process.

93
00:06:11,867 --> 00:06:16,667
It is to prove measurable value in one
real operational loop first.

94
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:19,467
Thank you for your time.
